<section class="idea-section not-content" id="the-idea">
  <div class="idea-container">
    <div class="idea-narrative-block">
      <div class="narrative-meta">
        <h2 class="idea-main-title">Why this engineering handbook is needed</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="narrative-content">
        <p class="lead-text">
          Agent security cannot be mastered from isolated vulnerability checklists. In an <strong>agentic system</strong>, language models no longer merely generate text; they make decisions in an iterative software loop, managing context, querying databases, running code, and triggering real-world side effects.
        </p>
        <p>
          Traditional software separates data from executable code. Because language models process both within a shared text stream, untrusted content can directly influence downstream control flow. Prompt engineering and content filters can mitigate simple failures, but they do not create a security boundary. Robust defense requires systemic architectural controls, credential scoping, sandbox isolation, and cryptographic provenance.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>You cannot secure an architecture you cannot explain.</strong> This handbook teaches in strict dependency order through two sequential passes: first, building a complete, verified mental model of the agentic system (from context sources and memory hierarchies to supervisors and tool protocols); second, revisiting that exact architecture with formal threat modeling, attack trees, defense in depth, and automated evaluation.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every chapter is grounded in official specifications, primary research, and minimal runnable examples, ensuring that engineering decisions rest on verifiable facts rather than hype.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

